    "q-118777": {
      "dom_id": "step-q118777",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{3})|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}a}{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot [0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{2\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2\\sqrt{2}}+\\frac{\\pi }{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
