    "q-118854": {
      "dom_id": "step-q118854",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+4}$ and the $x$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+4}.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe start by calculating the first of these integrals:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let $u={x}^{2}$, then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{u}{2}))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{{a}^{2}}{2})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+4}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
