    "q-347623": {
      "dom_id": "step-q347623",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14270639/assets/e203349d5d3b0d6b3bdd8a41e16017209ee175ee73c2010e17b8386d295c8b07.png)\n\nFind the total area $A$ of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=3{e}^{-|x|+2}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }3{e}^{-|x|+2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }3{e}^{-|x|+2}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{e}^{-|x|+2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }3{e}^{-|x|+2}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{e}^{-|x|+2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{e}^{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}3{e}^{x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{x+2}{|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{0+2}-{e}^{a+2}) \\\\  & =3({e}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $3{e}^{-|x|+2}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }3{e}^{-|x|+2}\\,\\text{d}x=3{e}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }3{e}^{-|x|+2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{e}^{-|x|+2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }3{e}^{-|x|+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}+3{e}^{2} \\\\  & =6{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
